Patch 27: run the trellis only on transform-type candidates that can still win.

Standalone, against anchor 5d628d8. Independent of patches 0025 and 0026 --
they act on the dry pass, this one acts everywhere.

THE FINDING BEHIND IT. A callgrind profile of this encoder puts trellis
quantisation at ~47% of instructions retired, and av2/encoder/trellis_quant.c
contains no speed-feature references at all. I previously described that as "no
speed feature reaches it". That was wrong in an informative way: two speed
features do govern per-block trellis, and both are switched off for the plane
that carries the cost.

In search_tx_type (av2/encoder/tx_search.c):

    // ~line 2411
    if (tcq_enable(cm->features.tcq_mode, is_lossless, plane, TX_CLASS_2D)) {
      perform_block_coeff_opt = 1;                    // unconditionally ON
    } else {
      perform_block_coeff_opt =                       // sf: perform_coeff_opt
          (block_mse_q8 <= coeff_opt_dist_threshold * qstep * qstep);
    }
    skip_trellis |= !perform_block_coeff_opt;

    // ~line 2588
    if (use_tcq) {
      skip_trellis_based_on_satd[tx_type] = skip_trellis;
    } else {
      skip_trellis_based_on_satd[tx_type] =           // sf: ..._based_on_satd
          skip_trellis_opt_based_on_satd(..., coeff_opt_satd_threshold, ...);
    }

tcq_enable() is true for luma when TCQ is on and the transform class is 2D --
and note the first call site passes TX_CLASS_2D as a literal, so for luma it is
true for every transform class. TCQ is on in the CTC configuration. So both
per-block trellis gates -- `perform_coeff_opt` (tuned 0 -> 2/3 -> 3/5 -> 4/6
across the presets) and `perform_coeff_opt_based_on_satd` (0 -> 1/2) -- are
live only for chroma, for 1D transform classes, and for lossless. Luma 2D, the
blocks that hold essentially all of the coefficient mass and all of the trellis
time, is exempt at every preset.

That is not an oversight to reverse. Under TCQ the decoder dequantises with the
state machine, so an encoder that quantises a block scalar-only and keeps its
scalar dqcoeff would drift. Forcing those gates back on for luma would be a
correctness bug, not a speed feature. The gates are off for a reason.

WHAT IS ACTUALLY WASTED. The waste is not that winners get trellised -- they
must be. It is that losers get trellised too. av2_optimize_b (and through it
av2_trellis_quant) is called inside the candidate loop, once per primary
transform type, per secondary-transform index, per transform block. Up to 16
primary types before pruning, times the IST/stx candidates. Exactly one of
those results is kept; the rest are computed at full 8-state cost and thrown
away.

The code already knows this test is worth making. Immediately after the trellis
it does:

    // If rd cost based on coeff rate alone is already more than best_rd,
    // terminate early.
    if (RDCOST(x->rdmult, rate_cost, 0) > best_rd) continue;

That check runs AFTER the trellis has been paid for, so it saves only the
distortion call. This patch runs the same idea BEFORE it.

WHAT THE PATCH DOES. After av2_quant has produced the FP-quantised
coefficients, cost them with cost_coeffs, take dist_block_tx_domain, and drop
the candidate before the trellis if that estimate is worse than the incumbent
by more than a margin.

The margin is not cosmetic. The estimate is pessimistic about the candidate:
the trellis exists to lower rate, so the candidate's true post-trellis RD is
below this figure, while best_rd is already post-trellis. Comparing them
directly would prune candidates that would have won. The shift sets how much
bias is forgiven -- the candidate is dropped only if it is worse than the
incumbent by more than 1/2^SHIFT of its own cost:

    -DAVM_TX_PRE_TRELLIS_GATE_SHIFT=2    drop only if 33% worse
    -DAVM_TX_PRE_TRELLIS_GATE_SHIFT=3    drop only if 14% worse   (default)
    -DAVM_TX_PRE_TRELLIS_GATE_SHIFT=4    drop only if  7% worse
    -DAVM_TX_PRE_TRELLIS_GATE_SHIFT=5    drop only if  3% worse
    -DAVM_TX_PRE_TRELLIS_GATE_SHIFT=-1   gate disabled (reproduces baseline)

64-point transforms are excluded (three coefficient quadrants are forced to
zero, so dist_block_tx_domain systematically mis-estimates them), as are FSC
blocks and eob == 0.

WHY THIS CANNOT CAUSE A MISMATCH. The gate only ever executes `continue` on a
losing candidate. It never changes how the winning candidate is quantised,
trellised, costed or reconstructed, and it modifies no state that survives the
iteration -- it runs before av2_optimize_b touches dqcoeff, eobs and
txb_entropy_ctx, so it perturbs strictly less than the existing continue two
statements later. The quality cost is confined to one channel: a candidate that
would have won may be dropped. Contrast patch 0025, which changed the
coefficients that were actually kept.

MEASURED LOCALLY (192x128, 4 frames, QP 185, cpu-used=3, three paired reps
against the same baseline binary):

    shift 2   -2.68%   (SLOWER: -3.44, -2.63, -1.98)
    shift 3   +7.73%   (+7.43, +7.86, +7.91)
    shift 4   +7.02%   (+6.93, +6.18, +7.97)

The shift-2 result is the informative one. The gate is not free: cost_coeffs
plus dist_block_tx_domain on every candidate costs about 2.7% of total encode
time, and at a 33% margin the gate fires too rarely to repay it. Everything
above shift 2 repays it several times over. Shifts 3 and 4 are within each
other's spread here, so this clip does not separate them.

All four variants produce distinct bitstreams and all decode cleanly with
avmdec.

WHAT TO RUN. Speed 3, two arms: shift 3 (default) and shift 4. Unlike 0025 this
change is not confined to the dry pass, so it is not expected to collapse at
Speed 4 the way 0025 did -- but Speed 3 is where the bar is closest and should
be answered first.

WHAT WOULD FALSIFY IT. The gate's whole premise is that a candidate whose
untrellised RD is far above the incumbent would not have won after trellising.
If BD-rate comes back above roughly 0.3% at shift 3, that premise is wrong --
the trellis is reordering candidates, not just improving them, and the correct
response is to raise the margin to shift 2 and accept that the gate cannot pay
for its own overhead, i.e. to retire the patch rather than tune it.

diff --git a/av2/encoder/tx_search.c b/av2/encoder/tx_search.c
index 2af0748..77131e4 100644
--- a/av2/encoder/tx_search.c
+++ b/av2/encoder/tx_search.c
@@ -2626,6 +2626,57 @@ static void search_tx_type(const AV2_COMP *cpi, MACROBLOCK *x, int plane,
                                    tx_sf->prune_intra_ist_stx_by_zero_eob)) {
           continue;
         }
+        // Pre-trellis RD gate.
+        //
+        // The trellis (RDOQ, or the 8-state TCQ path) is the single most
+        // expensive step in this loop -- a callgrind profile of the encoder
+        // puts trellis quantisation at ~47% of instructions retired -- and it
+        // runs once per candidate transform type, per secondary-transform
+        // index, per transform block. Only one candidate survives.
+        //
+        // The FP-quantised coefficients for this candidate are already sitting
+        // in qcoeff after av2_quant above. Cost them, take the transform-domain
+        // distortion, and drop the candidate before the trellis runs if even
+        // that estimate cannot come near the incumbent.
+        //
+        // The estimate is PESSIMISTIC about the candidate: the trellis exists
+        // to lower rate, so the true post-trellis RD is below this one, while
+        // best_rd is already a post-trellis figure. Comparing the two directly
+        // would prune candidates that would have won. The shift is the margin
+        // that pays for that bias -- the candidate is dropped only when it is
+        // worse than the incumbent by more than 1/2^SHIFT of its own cost.
+        //
+        //     shift 2  ->  drop only if 33% worse   (safest, least speed)
+        //     shift 3  ->  drop only if 14% worse   (default)
+        //     shift 4  ->  drop only if  7% worse
+        //     shift -1 ->  gate disabled entirely
+        //
+        // Note this is the same test the existing line below already performs
+        // ("if rd cost based on coeff rate alone is already more than best_rd,
+        // terminate early") -- but that one runs AFTER the trellis has been
+        // paid for, so it saves only the distortion call. This one runs before.
+        //
+        // Excluded: 64-point transforms, where three quadrants of coefficients
+        // are forced to zero and dist_block_tx_domain systematically
+        // mis-estimates; FSC blocks, which take a different optimize path; and
+        // eob == 0, which the trellis would not be called on anyway.
+#ifndef AVM_TX_PRE_TRELLIS_GATE_SHIFT
+#define AVM_TX_PRE_TRELLIS_GATE_SHIFT 3
+#endif
+#if AVM_TX_PRE_TRELLIS_GATE_SHIFT >= 0
+        if (quant_param.use_optimize_b && !fsc_mode_in && *eob != 0 &&
+            best_rd != INT64_MAX && txw != 64 && txh != 64) {
+          const int pre_rate = cost_coeffs(cm, x, plane, block, tx_size,
+                                           packed_tx_type, CCTX_NONE, txb_ctx,
+                                           cm->features.reduced_tx_set_used);
+          int64_t pre_dist, pre_sse;
+          dist_block_tx_domain(x, plane, block, tx_size, &pre_dist, &pre_sse);
+          const int64_t pre_rd = RDCOST(x->rdmult, pre_rate, pre_dist);
+          if (pre_rd - (pre_rd >> AVM_TX_PRE_TRELLIS_GATE_SHIFT) > best_rd) {
+            continue;
+          }
+        }
+#endif  // AVM_TX_PRE_TRELLIS_GATE_SHIFT >= 0
         if (fsc_mode_in && quant_param.use_optimize_b) {
           av2_optimize_fsc(cpi, x, plane, block, tx_size, packed_tx_type,
                            txb_ctx, &rate_cost);
